All five unbound-variable errors in `out/main-rB.workflow.json` are fixed. Buildcheck now prints `ok` with no errors, and the JSON loads.

**`{{title}}` and `{{last_name}}`:** Brief A lists these as variables the call starts with, but the workflow never declared them. I added a top-level `test_variables` block holding all five starting variables from the brief (`customer_id`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `title`, `now`). The values are placeholders I made up for testing (Ms Ann Lee, `c_test`, 2026-10-14T11:00), the same ones `main-rB4.workflow.json` uses. On a real call, the values passed in with the call replace them.

**`{{product}}`, `{{past_due}}` and `{{days_late}}`:** These only appeared in the system prompt's disclosure line. That line assumed a single product, but Brief B allows several products, each with its own amount and days late. So I didn't bind them to one value. Instead I reworded the line to use plain `<product>`, `<past_due>` and `<days_late>` slots, with an instruction to fill them from each product's `get_account` result, one sentence per late product. The script text is otherwise unchanged.

A hook's code (around line 649 of the file) still holds a fixed copy of the old sentence with the `{{...}}` placeholders. I left it alone because you said not to touch hook code, and the check doesn't scan code. If that text ever reaches the caller, it will still contain the raw placeholders.

Nothing else was changed, and nothing was saved to Freya.

